{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1458042-the-factory-technocratic-device","id":1458042,"title":"The Factory (Technocratic Device)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-08-30T02:33:59-07:00","id":1458042,"title":"The Factory (Technocratic Device)","content":"Something created after I had a convo with a friend about technocratic paradigm and technological level compared to the rest of the world around them. Agreement was that technocracy was roughly 100 to 150 years ahead of the rest of the world. Which meant that in the world of 1850s, you had year 2000 technology in the depths of the Technocratic Constructs. Aka, internet, electronics, you know the drill. <br /> <br /> This was interesting. <br /> <br /> <i>&quot;I'm going to kill Anthrun when I get out of here.&quot; Thought the Verbena, even as she dodged a deadly rain of flechette fire. The projectiles curved in the air, their miniature machine spirits thirsting for the blood of Gaia's chosen. But the blessings of the gods, obtained through pain and sacrifice and several tattoos onto her body, let her evade them. They exploded harmlessly into the air, shrapnel puncturing every surface except for her. She continued running. The entire area was a hellscape of pipes, walls, machinery, and shining chrome. She could barely hear her thoughts over the sound of clanging metal and the screams issuing from strange grille like technocratic machines. Machines that spat out lies and condemnations for her heresy and deviance, lies that would if she was any weaker would have droven her to her knees and left her catatonic. Instead, she fought on. <br /> <br /> This entire mission was a bust. The entire area was a death trap, meant to lure in fools and slay them. Obtaining that information that there was something valuable and important being done near Liverpool. There, puncturing the shroud the Order of Reason had placed within their large workshop was pierced easily enough when you know when to look. On hindsight, slaying the guard so easily should have been a warning. The Technocracy did not invest in fools. And yet she grew overconfident, believing them to have gotten complacent. And now she was in trouble. <br /> <br /> Spheres fell from the ceiling, from pipes above her, and began releasing gas, only her gifts preventing her from choking to death. Spikes, whips, and chains lashed out, only to be parried away by her dagger, solid steel alloy shattering like brittle clay. She threw out hexes and curses, words of life that would cripple and jam the un-living machinery of this place, that would blind the genius locii. She rounded a corner, going to where her instincts told her the exit would be. Lumbering giants walked out, heavy lumbering steps shaking the ground. Unlucky saps who were once men, they were now inhuman monsters swollen from alchemical concoctions and heavy metal bolted to their skin. They reached out at her. One slumped to the ground as it lost its head. The other grunted as it lost its arm. The third fell into a vat of acid as she slipped under it, using a gentle pivot to throw it head over heels. <br /> <br /> There, there was the exit. She slid across an oil puddle, adding more speed. Dodging. Moving. Pausing for a second to let the danger pass. Until the worst thing happened.to her. Her gifts, gifts from life itself, failed in this place. And as pipes pierced her limbs, she looked up to see a tub of hot lead tip over. <br /> <br /> It took hours for her to die. </i><br /> <br /> The Factory, or at least, Facility #728, was an old factory first built during 1850 near Liverpool, at [Redacted]. A specialized technocratic facility built to create first class weaponry and equipment for the armies of the Technocratic union, its quality of its goods was considered impeccable for Technocracy, a symbol of Technocratic science and industry over the old craftsmen style crafting of the medieval era. The place was a cavernous facility roughly several kilometers in area, filled to the brim with any kind of tool, material, or design. Capable of taking in raw iron ore and crafting them into cars, steam engines, kitchen cutlery, or I-beams. A combination of complex mechanical machinery, modular tooling, memory metals, as well as the wonders of electricity give the factory a previously unseen before flexibility in what it could create. <br /> <br /> Controlled by a single brain, along with numerous sub-brains. Species #983, a species of plant created by the Aesclupians for storage and preservation of specimens, altered to support the brain of an enlightened personnel who has given up his body for the cause. The nameless Technocrat's brain is kept in life support from a plant that converts electricity and light into food and chemical energy, keeping the brain alive. Connected by wires and nervous system mycelia, the brain is capable of controlling the miles of machinery within the area, keeping the entire facility running 24/7, churning out war material for the Technocratic machine. <br /> <br /> Workers exist here. But not just any workers. Malcontents, drifters, homeless, and orphans are given new life. Even the great machinery of the Factory cannot carry out all tasks. But these workers, freshly lobotomized and altered to fit their new role in life, given new purpose from their previously meaningless existence, can assist it. Their functions range from clearing up jams to sweeping up trash too small for machines to be used and too large to be ignored. Controlled by implants that have pre-recorded actions triggered by speakers or radio signals, these workers are vital part of any enterprise. And unlike real workers, they don't need to eat, sleep, nor protest. <br /> <br /> A/N: Just wanted to write this down. Might add more later on what this thing can actually do. It's basically an old style factory. Really weird, really steampunk, and also capable of cranking out enough mundane war material to make Ford ashamed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-30T06:12:03-07:00","id":1458049,"title":"","content":"Like the tone, it gives me some MECHA vive.<br /> <br /> It makes one think about how much you can get away with in the eartly realm exploiting reality zones.The product it's mundane, the larger Zone it's &quot;manufacturing&quot; (Which I'm guesing would ease production magick, and also some &quot;accidents&quot; &amp; some inhuman labor conditions), and the space tigthly controled. It's more supernatural denizens and stuff (like the disembodied brain-plants) could exist in sanctum-like places. Authorized personel only, of course.<br /> <br /> The whole human resource management may cause some well founded eyebrow twitching on the more &quot;idealist&quot; agents.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-30-2021, 09:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-30T07:16:51-07:00","id":1458052,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1458042-the-factory-technocratic-device?p=1458049#post1458049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like the tone, it gives me some MECHA vive.<br /> <br /> It makes one think about how much you can get away with in the eartly realm exploiting reality zones.The product it's mundane, the larger Zone it's &quot;manufacturing&quot; (Which I'm guesing would ease production magick, and also some &quot;accidents&quot; &amp; some inhuman labor conditions), and the space tigthly controled. It's more supernatural denizens and stuff (like the disembodied brain-plants) could exist in sanctum-like places. Authorized personel only, of course.<br /> <br /> The whole human resource management may cause some well founded eyebrow twitching on the more &quot;idealist&quot; agents.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mwahahahahahha. <br /> <br /> Inhumane? I assure you, there is nothing human inside there. The story was set in the past. There's only monsters in there. Whether human or otherwise. The supernatural denizens (the brain-plants) were early genetic hybrids created via mutagenesis in the order of reason, first used as life-support devices to help keep technocrats and tissue samples alive and in fresh condition. Just one of the many technological paths the technocracy never went down on. Like zeppelins/ blimps and atomic punk. <br /> <br /> On how much you can get away with? IIRC as long as you're in a construct all Technocratic procedures are considered to be mundane, while all Tradition rotes are considered vulgar (at least those that are outwardly magical). Technically to any observer in the 21st century this collection of unshielded electrical wires, hydraulics, and air powered machinery shouldn't work as well as it should, but it does. Fordism, Taylorism, Time-motion equations on those really old boards that would only be found in museums now, all combining to let steampunk technology do the work that wouldn't be out of place in the 20th century and beyond. This is Mundane production. Entropy for good quality and reliability, Time for speed of production (several factors, in fact). Matter for transmutation of elements (Yes, the etherites love this) and for high quality. Mind for artistry and boosting the central biological computer. <br /> <br /> After all, the power of the Technocracy comes from the <i>levee en masse</i>, and how could such armies march on the Reality Deviants when they are not clothed or equipped properly?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}